    "id": "xm8noq225igcjecq1c9m3tzj",
    "text": "Paragraph\n\nThe double-slit experiment is a classic demonstration of light’s wave-like behavior. When a beam of light passes through two narrow, closely spaced slits, it creates a pattern of alternating bright and dark bands on a screen. This interference pattern is difficult to explain if light is made only of particles, but it makes perfect sense if light behaves as a wave—waves from each slit overlap and combine, creating regions of constructive and destructive interference.\n\n+"
  },
