    "id": "cmsb6lbar002q3582g4bvew11",
    "text": "Process\n1\n2\n3\n1 of 5\nIntroduction to Double-Slit Interference\n\nThis lesson explores how light behaves when it passes through two narrow slits, demonstrating the wave nature of light through the formation of an interference pattern.\n\nSTART\n2 of 5\nStep 1\nLight Approaches the Slits\n\nA beam of light is directed toward a barrier with two narrow, parallel slits. The light spreads out as it reaches the slits.\n\n3 of 5\nStep 2\nPassing Through the Slits\n\nThe light waves pass through both slits, creating two sources of waves that spread out and overlap on the other side.\n\n4 of 5\nStep 3\nInterference Pattern Forms\n\nWhere the waves overlap, they combine—sometimes reinforcing each other (bright bands), sometimes canceling out (dark bands). This creates a series of alternating bright and dark stripes on the screen.\n\n5 of 5\nSummary: Evidence of Light's Wave Nature\n\nThe resulting pattern of bands is strong evidence that light behaves as a wave, since only waves can interfere in this way.\n\nSTART AGAIN\n+"
  },
