    "id": "cmsb6lbas002w3582nwbkbif5",
    "text": "Process\n1\n2\n3\n1 of 5\nIntroduction to Single-Photon Buildup Simulation\n\nThis lesson explores how individual photons behave in a double-slit experiment and how their collective behavior reveals the wave-particle duality of light.\n\nSTART\n2 of 5\nStep 1\nSingle Photon Emission\n\nLight is dimmed so that only one photon is emitted at a time toward the double slits.\n\n3 of 5\nStep 2\nIndividual Detection\n\nEach photon is detected as a single point of light on the screen behind the slits. At first, the dots seem randomly scattered.\n\n4 of 5\nStep 3\nPattern Emerges\n\nAs more photons are detected, a pattern begins to emerge. The dots cluster in regions that match the bright bands from the wave interference pattern.\n\n5 of 5\nSummary: Wave-Particle Duality Revealed\n\nEven though photons arrive one at a time, the overall pattern reveals wave-like interference, showing that light exhibits both particle and wave properties.\n\nSTART AGAIN\n+"
  },
