    "id": "tehx2p1m826rcmvqzz2clofh",
    "text": "Paragraph with heading\n\nThe Photoelectric Effect: Particle Evidence\n\nWhile interference patterns support the wave model, some experiments can only be explained if light acts as particles. The photoelectric effect is a key example: when light shines on certain metals, it can eject electrons from the surface—but only if the light’s frequency is high enough. This phenomenon led to the idea of photons: particles of light with energy determined by their frequency.\n\n+"
  },
