    "text": "Process\n1\n2\n3\n1 of 5\nIntroduction to the Photoelectric Effect Simulation\n\nThis lesson explores the photoelectric effect using a simulation. You will learn how changing the frequency and intensity of light affects the emission of electrons from a metal surface.\n\nSTART\n2 of 5\nStep 1\nShining Light on Metal\n\nLight of a chosen frequency and intensity is directed at a metal surface. You can adjust both the frequency (color) and the brightness (intensity) of the light.\n\n3 of 5\nStep 2\nObserving Electron Emission\n\nIf the light’s frequency is above a certain threshold, electrons are ejected from the metal. If the frequency is too low, no electrons are emitted, no matter how bright the light is.\n\n4 of 5\nStep 3\nChanging Intensity\n\nIncreasing the intensity (brightness) of high-frequency light causes more electrons to be emitted, but does not change their energy. For low-frequency light, increasing intensity still does not cause any electrons to be released.\n\n5 of 5\nSummary of the Photoelectric Effect\n\nThe photoelectric effect shows that light energy comes in packets (photons), and only photons with enough energy (high enough frequency) can eject electrons. This supports the particle model of light.\n\nSTART AGAIN\n+"
  },
