    "id": "3b335412-58ea-4592-a9f7-ba150c5486ac",
    "text": "Process\nMove up\n1\n2\n3\n4\n1 of 6\nWorked example: Can a photon eject an electron?\n\nA hypothetical surface has a work function of 2.00 eV. Compare 500 nm light with 700 nm light, using E ≈ 1240/λ when wavelength is in nanometres and energy is in electronvolts.\n\nSTART\n2 of 6\nStep 1\nCalculate the incident photon energy\n\nUse the energy of one photon. The work function is the minimum energy needed to remove an electron from this surface.\n\n3 of 6\nStep 2\nCompare energy with the threshold\n\nThe photon has more energy than the work function. Emission is possible; the remaining energy can become electron kinetic energy.\n\n4 of 6\nStep 3\nFind maximum kinetic energy\n\nSubtract the energy needed to remove the electron. This gives maximum kinetic energy, not the energy of every emitted electron.\n\n5 of 6\nStep 4\nTest a longer wavelength\n\nAt 700 nm, each photon has too little energy. Increasing photon flux still does not cause emission in this introductory single-photon model. The threshold depends on the material.\n\n6 of 6\nCheck the complete result\n\nA 500 nm photon can produce an electron with up to 0.48 eV of kinetic energy here. A 700 nm photon cannot overcome this surface’s work function.\n\nSTART AGAIN\n+"
  },
