    "id": "cmsb6lbau00393582tj4z0m5p",
    "text": "Tabs\nDOPPLER SHIFT\nCOLOR & MIXING\n\nThe Doppler shift occurs when a source of light moves toward or away from an observer. If the source moves toward you, the light’s frequency appears higher (blue shift); if it moves away, the frequency appears lower (red shift). This effect is used in astronomy to measure the motion of stars and galaxies.\n\n\n\n\nImportantly, the speed of light remains constant, even as the observed frequency changes. The color shift is due to changes in frequency, not speed.\n\n+"
  }
